=   entry .Event   what a connection logs: a request sent, and a `GOAWAY` received
-     detail
+     detail

-         Every `Event` is tagged `Log.Network` and `Log.Protocol`. `Connection#fetch` logs
-         `RequestSent(authority)` at `fine` — _sending an HTTP/2 request to `authority`_ — and a
-         connection's reader logs `GoAway(lastStream)` as a warning — _received GOAWAY; the last
-         processed stream was `lastStream`_ — before it stops. An `Http2.Event is Loggable` must
-         be in scope for a fetch; `logging.silentLogging` discards them.
-     entry .communicable   renders each event as a message
+         Todo `Event` lleva las etiquetas `Log.Network` y `Log.Protocol`. `Connection#fetch`
+         registra `RequestSent(authority)` en el nivel `fine` —_sending an HTTP/2 request to
+         `authority`_— y el lector de una conexión registra `GoAway(lastStream)` como un aviso
+         —_received GOAWAY; the last processed stream was `lastStream`_— antes de detenerse.
+         Debe haber un `Http2.Event is Loggable` en el ámbito para una petición;
+         `logging.silentLogging` los descarta.
+     entry .communicable   representa cada evento como un mensaje

- entry .Hpack   an HPACK codec: compresses and decompresses HTTP/2 header blocks with a dynamic table of its own
+ entry .Hpack   un códec HPACK: comprime y descomprime los bloques de cabeceras de HTTP/2 con una tabla dinámica propia

= entry .Hpack   an HPACK codec: compresses and decompresses HTTP/2 header blocks with a dynamic table of its own
-   detail
+   detail

-       HPACK (RFC 7541) is how HTTP/2 carries headers: each field is an index into a table of
-       recently-seen fields, or a literal name and value, each Huffman-coded where that is
-       shorter. An `Hpack` instance owns one direction's dynamic table, which changes with every
-       block it encodes or decodes, so a connection keeps one codec for decoding inbound blocks
-       and one for encoding outbound ones, and the two never mix.
+       HPACK (RFC 7541) es la forma en que HTTP/2 lleva las cabeceras: cada campo es un índice en
+       una tabla de campos vistos recientemente, o un nombre y un valor literales, cada uno
+       codificado con Huffman cuando así resulta más corto. Una instancia de `Hpack` posee la
+       tabla dinámica de un sentido, que cambia con cada bloque que codifica o decodifica, así
+       que una conexión mantiene un códec para decodificar los bloques entrantes y otro para
+       codificar los salientes, y los dos nunca se mezclan.

=       and one for encoding outbound ones, and the two never mix.
